<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Phase-3 extra Info.plist entries merged on top of Xcode's generated Info.plist
		(GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE=YES and INFOPLIST_FILE both set): Xcode merges this file's
		keys in. It carries ONLY the keys that cannot be expressed as INFOPLIST_KEY_* build
		setting, chiefly the `mattermost://` custom URL scheme consumed by DeepLinkRouter and
		the App Transport Security (ATS) + privacy keys below (T18 App Store readiness gate).

		The desktop app declares the `mattermost` scheme (and a dev `mattermost-beta` scheme);
		registering it here lets links opened in Safari/other apps route back into this client.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.mattermost.ios</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>mattermost</string>
				<string>mattermost-beta</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
		ATS (App Transport Security), scoped — T18 release gate. This is a WKWebView shell:
		the chat UI is the Mattermost web app, and the product lets the user point the app at
		any Mattermost server they configure (self-hosted, enterprise, or on a LAN / dev box).
		Two narrow relaxations, nothing global:

		1. NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent = true — relaxes ATS ONLY inside WKWebView web
		   content (the officially sanctioned key for webview-host apps). It does NOT relax ATS
		   for the app's own URLSession layer: native shell networking (server-info probes,
		   downloads, diagnostics) still requires https and valid TLS. Apple's ATS
		   documentation explicitly directs webview apps to use this key instead of
		   NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. Without it, a self-hosted Mattermost server reachable only
		   over plain http (common for LAN/transitional deployments) could never render even
		   after the user configures it. NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is deliberately NOT set.

		2. NSAllowsLocalNetworking = true — lets the shell validate/add self-hosted or
		   on-network servers reachable over http:// on the local network (e.g. a plain-HTTP
		   Mattermost instance on a LAN, or the local stub servers the XCUITest suite drives)
		   without disabling ATS for the whole app — public-internet connections still require
		   https. Private-range addresses (10.x, 172.16-31.x, 192.168.x) and .local count as
		   local networking under ATS.

		Runtime scoping: the shell only ever instantiates a WKWebView for servers present in
		ServerStore (RootView.activate), and ServerURLValidator normalizes user-entered
		addresses to https unless the user explicitly types an http:// URL. TLS failures on
		https servers are handled by CertTrust (user-confirmed per origin, Keychain-persisted),
		never silently bypassed. See docs/app-store-review-notes.md for the full narrative.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!--
		Permission rationale strings. Camera/microphone are NOT used by native code today;
		they are required by WKWebView's getUserMedia so the Mattermost web app's Calls
		feature can request the device camera/mic when the user starts a call inside the
		hosted web app. Local-network covers connecting to LAN self-hosted dev servers
		(modelled by NSAllowsLocalNetworking above).
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mattermost needs camera access for video calls you start in the Mattermost web app.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mattermost needs microphone access for voice and video calls you start in the Mattermost web app.</string>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Mattermost connects to Mattermost servers on your local network, for example a self-hosted development server on your LAN.</string>
	<!--
		Export-compliance declaration: the app only uses HTTPS/TLS (exempt) and performs no
		other cryptography; App Store Connect's export-compliance question can therefore be
		answered "no encryption" without a per-build review. Revisit if the app ever ships
		its own crypto.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
		T14 Background App Refresh (Option A notifications). Two plain Info.plist keys —
		verified against the BackgroundTasks SDK headers and Apple's "Configuring your app
		for background tasks" documentation they need NO paid Apple capability or
		entitlement (unlike APNs, which requires a Push Notifications entitlement bound to
		a paid Apple Developer Program account). They are:

		  UIBackgroundModes = fetch
		    The classic background-fetch mode; required for BGAppRefreshTask launches
		    (BGTaskScheduler documents "fetch" as the mode its refresh tasks need).

		  BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers = [com.mattermost.ios.refresh]
		    The task identifier the scheduler is allowed to launch. Must match
		    BackgroundRefreshScheduler.taskIdentifier exactly; a task whose identifier
		    is not listed here is silently never launched.

		See docs/t14-build-note.md for the lldb simulation procedure
		(_simulateLaunchForTaskWithIdentifier:) that exercises the task on the simulator.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.mattermost.ios.refresh</string>
	</array>
	<!--
		Sentry DSN (T19a). The Sentry handler reads this key; an empty/missing value means the
		no-op path: SentrySDK.start is never called (verified by unit test). Set a real DSN
		here the day the Sentry Cocoa SDK is linked into the target (see SentrySDKLink.swift
		+ docs/t19a-build-note.md). Never commit a real DSN for a public release build.
	-->
	<key>SentryDSN</key>
	<string></string>
</dict>
</plist>
